In addition to the following response from Peter Reynolds, I received
responses from Joe Fletcher, Antonio Urbano and Bryan Lavelle =
confirming
that the processors are RISC.=20
------------------------------------------------------------------------=
----
----------------------------------
RISC is an acronym standing for Reduced Instruction Set Computing, and =
is a
type of CPU. Alpha processors are RISC processors, as are MIPS =
processors,
SUN processors, IBM processors. I think in your question it basically =
refers
to all non Intel CPU's. The EV56 refers to a certain family of Alpha =
chip
(Earlier processors were EV4, EV5, newer processors are EV6, EV67).
=20
Also got this response from Joe Ryals:
There is 2 basic types of processors RISC (reduced instruction set =
chip)
based
and CISC (complex instruction set chip) based. Alpha Processors are of =
the
RISC
based variety as is IBM's RISC 6000's, Sun Microsystems Ultra chips, =
etc.
Intel, Motorola's 68xx and 68xxx line, AMD, etc are based on CISC
architecture
and is commonly referred to as x86 based processors. Sorry to go into =
more
detail than it is this or that but now you can have a better =
understanding
of
the 2 types of architectures used in the industry.

Thanks everyone.

